## Benchmarking DuckDB v2.0-dev on NVIDIA Vera {#blog:2026-10-16-nvidia-vera-benchmark}

**Publication date:** 2026-10-16

**Author:** Gábor Szárnyas

**TL;DR:** We put NVIDIA's new Vera CPU through its paces using the full TPC-H workload on scale factor 1,000.



We love running benchmarks on all sorts of new hardware.
This often includes
[somewhat](https://duckdb.org/everywhere/raspberry-pi-5/)
[exotic](https://duckdb.org/everywhere/iphone-17-pro/)
[hardware](https://duckdb.org/everywhere/morefine-m700s/) –
where we sometimes need to compile a new DuckDB binary.
This time, in collaboration with the NVIDIA team,
we got our hands on their new Vera chip, aimed at agentic workloads.

[NVIDIA Vera](https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/data-center/vera-cpu/) comes with 88 “Olympus” cores, Spatial Multithreading and
up to 1.2 terabytes per second (TB/s) of memory bandwidth.
What's more, Vera runs arm64 binaries – so it runs DuckDB's regular build without any modifications.

To see how this new hardware performs, we ran the full TPC-H benchmark (not just the read queries!) on scale factor 1,000.
Our setup included two server platforms: an x86 baseline (Intel Xeon 6) and NVIDIA Vera.

#### Environment {#blog:2026-10-16-nvidia-vera-benchmark::environment}

##### System Setup {#blog:2026-10-16-nvidia-vera-benchmark::system-setup}

Both machines ran Ubuntu 26.04.
Their hardware included 768 GB of memory and a single NVMe SSD with 3+ TB of storage, formatted as ext4.
At the time of writing, DuckDB v2.0 was [not released yet](#blog:2026-09-02-try-duckdb-20-alpha), so we used a [preview build](https://duckdb.org/install/?version=preview), `1.6.0.dev379`.

To run the experiments, we used the [`duckdb-tpch` benchmark harness](https://github.com/duckdb/duckdb-tpch/).
To re-create our benchmark environment, see our script below.



<details markdown='1'>
<summary markdown='span'>
Click here to reveal the setup script.
</summary>
```bash
# make sure you have the required tools
sudo apt install git g++ cmake python3-pip

# clone the repository at the commit used for the experiments
git clone https://github.com/duckdb/duckdb-tpch
cd duckdb-tpch
git reset --hard cf096cea8a0a054cefe0e695c842ca5bca6ec04d

# install the required Python dependencies
python3 -m venv venv
source venv/bin/activate
python3 -m pip install -r requirements.txt
python3 -m pip install duckdb==1.6.0.dev379

# build the dbgen tool
cd tpch_tools_3.0.1/dbgen/
make
cd ../..

# generate the dataset, query streams and update streams
export SF=1000
./generate.sh
```
</details>



> **Tip.** To generate the large-scale TPC-H dataset efficiently,
> the repository uses the wonderful [`tpchgen-cli` tool](https://pypi.org/project/tpchgen-cli/),
> a parallel Rust rewrite of the TPC-H data generator.
> It also uses the old generator to produce update and query streams.

This workload is relatively large-scale (SF1,000) and highly concurrent during the throughput run.
To get the best performance out of DuckDB, we set the following flags:

```sql
SET block_allocator_memory = '500G';
SET allocator_background_threads = true;
```

##### x86 Baseline: Intel Xeon 6 {#blog:2026-10-16-nvidia-vera-benchmark::x86-baseline-intel-xeon-6}

The x86 baseline setup used an Intel Xeon 6 CPU.
The CPU had 96 cores with [hyper-threading](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hyper-threading),
yielding 192 threads.
However, we found that DuckDB performs best when using 96 threads, so we configured it accordingly:

```sql
SET threads = 96;
```

##### NVIDIA Vera {#blog:2026-10-16-nvidia-vera-benchmark::nvidia-vera}

The second machine had the NVIDIA Vera CPU, which packs 88 cores with [Spatial Multithreading](https://developer.nvidia.com/blog/inside-nvidia-vera-cpu-olympus-cores-built-for-maximum-single-threaded-performance-in-agentic-ai/).
We conducted the benchmark on a dual-socket system but restricted DuckDB to a single socket using the `numactl` command:

```bash
numactl --cpunodebind=0 --membind=0 python3 benchmark.py
```

We also pinned the number of threads:

```sql
SET threads = 88;
```

#### Benchmark Runs and Results {#blog:2026-10-16-nvidia-vera-benchmark::benchmark-runs-and-results}

We performed an initial warmup run and discarded its result.
Then, we conducted three complete runs in sequence and took the median value of the _QphH@Size_ composite scores:

|                  | x86 QphH@Size    | Vera QphH@Size   |
| ---------------- | ---------------- | ---------------- |
| Run 1            | 2,120,588.82     | 3,073,292.99     |
| Run 2            | 2,022,795.01     | 3,131,306.27     |
| Run 3            | 2,109,782.19     | 3,052,102.87     |
| **Median score** | **2,109,782.19** | **3,073,292.99** |

The results show that the Vera platform has a 46% performance advantage on TPC-H SF1,000 over the x86 baseline (Intel Xeon 6).
In fact, if we were to audit these results through the official TPC-H channels, both would stand a chance to be in the top 3 among the [TPC-H Top Performance Results](https://www.tpc.org/tpch/results/tpch_perf_results5.asp?resulttype=noncluster&version=4) on SF1,000.

We attribute the performance advantage of Vera to three key factors.
First, the high memory bandwidth enables fast scans.
Second, combined with low memory latency, it keeps the cores continuously occupied during processing.
Finally, the Olympus cores' large caches allow efficient access to the hash tables built during aggregations and joins.

We are looking forward to continuing this benchmark by using DuckDB v2.0.0's stable build, running on the full dual-socket setup and incorporating different benchmarks.


## Announcing DuckDB 2.0.0 LTS “Cinnamon Teal” {#blog:2026-10-21-announcing-duckdb-200}

**Publication date:** 2026-10-21

**Author:** The DuckDB team

**TL;DR:** The DuckDB team is happy to announce that today we're releasing DuckDB version 2.0.0 LTS, codenamed “Cinnamon Teal” (Spatula cyanoptera). This is our biggest feature release so far, and it ships a polished version of the Quack protocol, asynchronous I/O, a first-class `VARIANT` type, agent mode, triggers, an extensible SQL parser and much more, built from close to 20,000 commits since DuckDB 1.5.

We are proud to release DuckDB 2.0.0, codenamed [“Cinnamon Teal”](https://shell.duckdb.org/#queries=v0,ATTACH-'https%3A%2F%2Fblobs.duckdb.org%2Fduck.db'-AS-duckdb~,CALL-duckdb.cinnamon_teal()~) after the [_Spatula cyanoptera_](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cinnamon_teal), a strikingly reddish-brown duck found in the western Americas.

While [DuckDB 1.0](#blog:2024-06-03-announcing-duckdb-100) was all about stability and backward compatibility, DuckDB 2.0 is above all a feature release.
In this blog post, we summarize the highlights and then walk through the breaking changes and how to migrate.
We showcased many of the new features in [“A Preview of DuckDB v2.0”](#blog:2026-08-17-duckdb-20-highlights), which covers each one in more depth.
For the complete release notes, see the [release page on GitHub](https://github.com/duckdb/duckdb/releases/tag/v2.0.0).

> **Tip.** To install the new version, please visit the [installation page](https://duckdb.org/install).

With this release, there are currently three DuckDB lines available.
DuckDB 2.0 is a long-term support (LTS) release with support until at least October 2027.
The previous LTS release, version 1.4, is still supported until November 17, 2026.
To ensure a smooth transition and allow sufficient time for testing, we extended the support period of DuckDB 1.5 until March 2027, effectively also turning it into an LTS release.
For details, see the [release calendar](https://duckdb.org/release_calendar#lts-releases).

To find your way around the documentation for all of the new features, we also published a [“What's New in the Docs for 2.0”](https://duckdb.org/docs/2.0/whats_new_in_the_docs) page that links each feature below to its documentation.

#### DuckDB as a Server {#blog:2026-10-21-announcing-duckdb-200::duckdb-as-a-server}

DuckDB has been an in-process database since day one.
In [DuckDB 1.5.3](#blog:2026-05-20-announcing-duckdb-153), released in May, we introduced the Quack protocol that lets you run DuckDB in a traditional client-server setup, along with the [`quack` extension](https://duckdb.org/docs/2.0/core_extensions/quack), which implements DuckDB's native protocol, so any DuckDB process can [serve its databases](https://duckdb.org/docs/2.0/quack/overview#server-side-usage).

##### Quack and `CONNECT` {#blog:2026-10-21-announcing-duckdb-200::quack-and-connect}

DuckDB 2.0.0 ships a polished version of the Quack protocol with substantial performance and stability improvements. It also introduces the new [`CONNECT` statement](https://duckdb.org/docs/2.0/sql/statements/connect) that allows attaching and switching to a remote database in a single command for better ergonomics:



<div class="duck-diagram-box" markdown="1">

###### <svg class="icon"><use href="#database-01"></use></svg> DuckDB server {#blog:2026-10-21-announcing-duckdb-200::svg-classiconuse-hrefdatabase-01usesvg-duckdb-server}

```sql
CALL quack_serve(
    token = 'my_token'
);

CREATE TABLE events
    (date DATE, name VARCHAR);

INSERT INTO events VALUES
    ('2027-04-07',
     'DuckCon #8');
```

quack:

###### <svg class="icon"><use href="#database-01"></use></svg> DuckDB client {#blog:2026-10-21-announcing-duckdb-200::svg-classiconuse-hrefdatabase-01usesvg-duckdb-client}

```sql
ATTACH 'quack:localhost'
    AS qk (TOKEN 'my_token');

CONNECT qk;
SELECT * FROM events;

┌────────────┬────────────┐
│    date    │    name    │
│    date    │  varchar   │
├────────────┼────────────┤
│ 2027-04-07 │ DuckCon #8 │
└────────────┴────────────┘
```

</div>



For simplicity, this example runs the server and the client on the same machine. By default, the server only listens on `localhost`. To accept connections from other machines, keep the server on `localhost` and put it behind a [TLS-terminating reverse proxy](https://duckdb.org/docs/2.0/quack/setup/reverse_proxy). The [`allow_other_hostname` option](https://duckdb.org/docs/2.0/quack/overview#starting-a-server), which exposes the server directly, is only meant for testing.

The `CONNECT` statement is not limited to Quack: it points your session at [any remote database that supports it](https://duckdb.org/docs/2.0/sql/statements/connect#supported-databases), including PostgreSQL and MySQL. For these, the new remote pushdown optimizer ships queries directly to the remote server instead of pulling tables over the wire.

##### Quack in the CLI {#blog:2026-10-21-announcing-duckdb-200::quack-in-the-cli}

The CLI also has shortcuts for setting up a Quack client-server pair: the new [`-serve` and `-connect` arguments](https://duckdb.org/docs/2.0/clients/cli/arguments#serving-and-connecting-to-remote-databases) start a Quack server or connect to one directly from the command line:



